.lua
book.chapter(26, "Graph algorithms and dependency analysis")
.endlua
.sh 2 "State the meaning of an edge"
.pp
Graph represents directed relationships using pairs of string node names.
Before applying an algorithm, define the edge orientation. An edge from a
prerequisite to its consumer supports prerequisite-first topological order.
An edge from a consumer to its dependency encodes the opposite relation.
Both are legitimate models, but silently switching conventions produces build
plans in the wrong order even when the graph algorithm is correct.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[edges := [("parse", "check"), ("check", "emit"), ("parse", "emit")]
Test.equal "dependency order" (Graph.topo_sort [] edges) (Ok ["parse", "check", "emit"])
Test.equal "direct shortest path" (Graph.shortest_path "parse" "emit" edges)
  (Some ["parse", "emit"])
Test.equal "reverse unreachable" (Graph.shortest_path "emit" "parse" edges) None]=],
    "Edges point from prerequisites to consumers")
.endlua
.pp
Some functions accept an explicit node list in addition to edges. This retains
isolated vertices that cannot be discovered from endpoints. A project with a
standalone source file still contains that file even if it has no dependencies.
Other operations discover nodes in deterministic first-seen order. Include
isolated-node cases in tests so an otherwise correct traversal does not
silently omit valid work.
.sh 2 "Reachability answers impact questions"
.pp
Successors and predecessors inspect adjacent nodes. Reachable performs a
breadth-first traversal and includes the starting node. Depth_first provides
another deterministic traversal order. With prerequisite-to-consumer edges,
reachability from a changed input identifies potentially affected consumers.
This is an impact set, not necessarily a minimal rebuild set: content hashes
or semantic equivalence may establish that some downstream outputs are
unchanged despite being reachable.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[edges := [("a", "b"), ("b", "c"), ("a", "d")]
Test.equal "adjacent consumers" (Graph.successors "a" edges) ["b", "d"]
Test.equal "adjacent prerequisites" (Graph.predecessors "c" edges) ["b"]
Test.equal "breadth first" (Graph.reachable "a" edges) ["a", "b", "d", "c"]
Test.check "self edge is cyclic" (Graph.has_cycle [("x", "x")])]=],
    "Adjacency and transitive impact")
.endlua
.pp
Shortest_path is unweighted. It minimizes the number of edges, not a numeric
cost attached to a domain operation. For an explanation such as "why does
this target depend on this input?" an unweighted shortest path is often useful.
For scheduling by elapsed time or monetary cost, a different algorithm and
representation are needed. The function's name does not imply a weighted
optimization problem that its input type cannot even express.
.sh 2 "Topological sorting exposes cycles"
.pp
A topological order places every source before its destination. It exists
only for an acyclic directed graph. Topo_sort returns Err when a cycle
prevents such an order. Multiple valid orders can exist; deterministic output
for a fixed input does not make one order mathematically unique. Normalize
externally obtained node and edge lists when reproducibility must survive
unstable directory enumeration or native hash-map iteration.
.lua
book.algorithm("Kahn-style topological ordering", {
    "Compute the incoming-edge count of every node.",
    "Place all zero-count nodes in a deterministic ready queue.",
    "While the queue is nonempty:",
    {1, "Remove a ready node and append it to the output."},
    {1, "Decrease the incoming counts of its successors."},
    {1, "Enqueue newly zero-count successors in a stable order."},
    "If output omits nodes, report a cycle; otherwise return the order."
})
.endlua
.pp
The pseudocode explains the invariant: a ready node has no unprocessed
predecessor. Each processed edge removes one outstanding requirement. A
remaining nonempty subgraph with no ready node contains a dependency cycle.
This reasoning is useful even if the native implementation uses different
internal containers. It connects the algorithm's output to the meaning of
the edges rather than merely presenting a traversal recipe.
.sh 2 "Strong components organize mutual dependence"
.pp
Strongly connected components partition nodes into maximal groups whose
members can reach one another. The sccs operation accepts an explicit node
list, retaining isolated vertices. A singleton component is cyclic only if
it has a self-edge. In a language implementation, components can group
mutually dependent definitions before type analysis; in a build planner,
they can provide a more informative cycle report than a single boolean.
.pp
Collapse each component to one summary node and the resulting component
graph is acyclic. That observation separates local cyclic reasoning from
global scheduling. It does not automatically make cyclic module initialization
valid: a group can still request a value before it exists. Graph analysis
identifies structure; the language's initialization semantics determine
whether that structure has an executable interpretation.
.sh 2 "Visualize the model you actually use"
.lua
book.graph({
    {id="P", label="parse", x=0, y=0},
    {id="C", label="check", x=2.3, y=0},
    {id="E", label="emit", x=4.6, y=0}
}, {{"P", "C"}, {"C", "E"}}, "A prerequisite-first language pipeline")
.endlua
.pp
Graph.to_dot produces Graphviz source rather than launching an external
renderer. This separation makes visualization usable from an embedding that
wants to store, inspect, or transmit the graph. The book's diagrams instead
use its custom pic-producing rofflet, but the same principle applies: the
data model and its rendering are separate operations. Label the orientation
and include isolated nodes so a picture does not accidentally misrepresent
the graph being analyzed.
.pp
The MetaML Graph API rebuilds native graph structures from its lists for
operations; it is not a persistent mutable graph handle. Repeated analysis
of a very large graph can therefore incur conversion and allocation costs.
The native DSLtk DirectedGraph API offers a different interface with stable
handles, discussed in chapter 38. Select the boundary appropriate to the
workload rather than assuming the two APIs have identical resource behavior.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metalib/cpp/Graph.h and its D mirror, then compare the DirectedGraph
implementation in DSLtk.hpp. Build a dependency report with one isolated
node, one diamond, and one cycle. Explain why reversing every edge preserves
the existence of cycles but reverses the intended scheduling interpretation.
Add a shortest-path explanation for one dependency and a component-based
diagnostic for the cycle.
